### LOCAL STORAGE SYSTEM — CRITICAL

The app must use **local device storage as the primary and persistent storage system**, especially in the Android APK.

#### Android APK Storage Setup

- On the first launch, BEFORE the app starts normally, show a clean storage setup screen.

- Ask the user to **select the main storage folder/location** where the entire Notes library will live.

- Request only the storage/file permissions actually required by the selected Android storage API.

- Remember the selected folder and use it as the application's single storage root.

- Allow the user to change the storage location later from Storage & Data Settings.

#### Storage Structure

Everything must be stored inside the selected main folder:

- Notes.

- Folders.

- Tags/metadata.

- Ordering/state data.

- `notes.md` files.

- Images.

- GIFs.

- All other attachments.

- Export/import data where applicable.

Each note must have its own properly organized local folder, with its note content and attachments stored together. Preserve folder hierarchy and note relationships exactly.

#### Important

- Do NOT make IndexedDB/LocalStorage the authoritative storage for the Android APK.

- The selected local device folder must be the **source of truth** for Android.

- On restart, reopen the library from that selected folder.

- Never silently switch to another storage location.

- Handle missing, moved, inaccessible, or permission-revoked folders gracefully.

- Never silently delete or overwrite existing user data.

- Autosave changes directly to the local storage system.

#### Web Compatibility

Keep the same architecture and data model for the web version, but respect browser filesystem/security restrictions. Do not pretend a normal browser can access arbitrary phone folders. Use the appropriate browser-supported local storage/file APIs while keeping the same storage abstraction.

**ABSOLUTE RULE:** The APK and web app must use the same application architecture and data model, while the storage layer uses the appropriate platform API. The Android APK must treat the user's selected local folder as the persistent source of truth.

### STORAGE SYNCHRONIZATION — CRITICAL

The local storage must remain the **single source of truth** and must stay synchronized with the UI at all times.

Whenever the user:

- Changes a note title.

- Edits note content.

- Adds an image or GIF.

- Removes an image or GIF.

- Reorders attachments.

- Changes the hero/cover image.

- Changes folders, tags, colors, or other note metadata.

the corresponding local files and metadata must be **immediately and reliably updated**.

The app must never create a stale copy that becomes disconnected from the main note.

- Update the existing note's storage location rather than accidentally creating a duplicate note.

- If the title changes, safely update the note's stored name/folder reference while preserving all content and attachments.

- If an attachment changes, update only the affected attachment while preserving the rest of the note.

- Keep `notes.md`, attachments, metadata, indexes, and UI state synchronized.

- Use atomic/transactional writes where possible to prevent corruption during interrupted saves.

- Autosave every change reliably.

- After every update, verify that reopening the note, refreshing the app, or restarting the APK shows the latest state.

**ABSOLUTE RULE:** Editing a note must never break, detach, duplicate, overwrite incorrectly, or lose the main stored note or any of its attachments.



